--TEST--
`int $x;` declares without binding: the type is the declaration's and the value arrives later, on one line or on every branch of an `if` — `rule:types/declaration`, `rule:types/var-inference`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:statements/expression-statements-blocks-and-declarations
// `rule:types/declaration` fixes a binding's type where it is declared, and nothing about
// that needs an initializer on the same line: the definite-assignment pass
// refuses a *read* on a path no assignment reached, which is what makes a
// bare declaration safe rather than a hole. What it must not do is let the
// first assignment decide the representation instead — the `?string` below is
// where that shows, since one branch assigns a string and the other assigns
// `null`, and the two agree at the join only because the declaration already
// said what the slot is. A bare `;` is a statement in the same sense: legal
// wherever a statement is, and it does nothing.
class Counter {
    public static function run(int $seed): string {
        // Declared at the top and assigned below, the shape a body written
        // for definite assignment naturally takes.
        string $label;
        int $total;
        $total = 0;
        int $k;
        for ($k = 0; $k < 3; $k++) {
            $total = $total + $seed;
        }
        $label = "total=";
        return $label . ($total as string);
    }
}

int $i;
$i = 2;
;
echo $i, "\n";

// Both branches are the *first* assignment, and they disagree about the value
// while agreeing about the slot.
?string $maybe;
if ($i == 2) {
    $maybe = "here";
} else {
    $maybe = null;
}
if ($maybe != null) {
    echo $maybe, "\n";
}

?string $absent;
if ($i == 3) {
    $absent = "no";
} else {
    $absent = null;
}
echo ($absent ?? "fell back"), "\n";

// A declaration inside a method body, read after a loop has written it.
echo Counter::run(2), "\n";
--EXPECT--
2
here
fell back
total=6
